WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:13.010
Our first panelist is professor of linguistics
and Executive Director of the Center for Teaching

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:22.930
Research and Learning at American University.
Please welcome Naomi Baron.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:28.461
Next is the Paulette Goddard chair of digital
media and learning sciences and a professor

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:34.410
in the Steinhardt School of Culture Education
and Human Development here at NYU. Please

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.940
welcome Jan Plass.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:42.430
Also joining us is associate professor of
child and adolescent psychiatry at NYU School

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:47.010
of Medicine and clinical psychologist and
neuropsychologist at the Child Study Center

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:51.480
of the NYU Langone Medical Center. Please
welcome Dr. Richard Gallagher.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:57.570
Last but not least we have a postdoctoral
fellow in the department of psychology at

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:02.940
Temple University. She's also a visiting associate
assistant professor at Haverford College.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.350
Please welcome Lauren Sherman.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:15.590
So we start with a couple of givens. We're
all social creatures as humans. And recent

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:20.480
research suggests that social interaction
in small and larger groups has media play

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:27.160
a key role in our evolution and survival and
fanning out across the planet. But that all

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:33.200
happened in a very different environmental
landscape. We know that in other cases take

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.149
the obesity epidemic in the United States
where we were evolved not to live in a land

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:44.340
of Coca-Cola and french fries. There's there's
consequences when the nature of the environment

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:50.349
around us change changes. And for sure that
that's happened with social interaction with

00:01:50.349 --> 00:01:56.170
digital media and online platforms. So first
from each of you I'd love to hear what you

00:01:56.170 --> 00:02:00.770
think the headline is about how social and
digital media are affecting young people in

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:06.770
particular as they grow up in the world today.
Let's start with you Naomi. You've done research

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:12.320
on how social media is affecting children
and teenagers reading and in other affects.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.719
What's your biggest headline finding

00:02:15.719 --> 00:02:20.180
The biggest finding, as far as I can tell
now, and I'm saying that because we really

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:28.230
don't have nearly enough research, is we assume
that reading digitally is no different from

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:37.110
reading in print. We don't know that for a
fact. My major concern is that the devices

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:42.330
on which we do our digital reading are devices
that were designed for very different kinds

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:49.220
of activity and we can call it interactive
activity sometimes socially interactive sometimes

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:55.379
interactive with sites but it's activity that
leads you to spend a very small amount of

00:02:55.379 --> 00:03:01.220
time on it on a particular screen where it
leads you to multitask. It doesn't lead you

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:06.209
to sit back and concentrate which is what
in principle, not always in practice, but

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:12.310
in principle print allowed us to do.So my
major concern my headline is until we really

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:20.760
know how to train people to take the mindset
of reading in print and use that reading digitally

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.760
we're going to be incredibly shallow readers
and there's some studies that have been done

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:28.040
that can suggest that's what's happening.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.459
Lauren you've studied the effects of social
media on the teenager and adult in real time.

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:36.450
Can you describe your really interesting experiments?

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:41.510
Yes so it was really surprising to me that
up until very recently nobody had actually

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:45.769
looked to see what's going on in the brain
when adolescents or really people of any age

00:03:45.769 --> 00:03:50.610
were using social media. And so we have all
these questions. We want to know what are

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:54.069
the effects of social media. What are the
effects of technology on the brain and so

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:58.400
you think a very first step would just be
to say alright let's put someone in the MRI

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:03.219
scanner and let's see what's going on in their
brain when they use social media? And maybe

00:04:03.219 --> 00:04:07.870
the reason that it wasn't done is because
it's actually not so simple because if we

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.569
think about social media there are many different
things right and many experiences that you

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:18.850
have whether it's on Facebook or writing a
blog are indeed very complex. And so as much

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:22.610
as it's nice that you can be in the MRI scanner
and you see a little screen in front of you

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:26.960
we like to have experiments in the MRI that
are very simple they are very controlled.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:32.039
And so it actually was a little bit hard to
get from this really rich complex experience

00:04:32.039 --> 00:04:36.920
on your computer or your phone to something
that we can look at in the scanner and what

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:40.430
was really exciting to me when I was trying
to think about OK how do we do this? Where

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:44.099
did we start? Right around the time that I
was thinking about this question Instagram

00:04:44.099 --> 00:04:48.120
started to become really popular with teens.
And the thing that's cool about Instagram

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:53.610
is it takes what previously was very complicated,
if you think about Facebook that you have

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:58.780
several feeds at the same time lots of text,
Instagram suddenly makes it very simple. So

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:02.600
you have a picture. You have a little bit
of information about that picture, maybe the

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.690
likes and the number of comments.

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:09.639
And so the question that my colleagues and
I decided to look at is say you're looking

00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:14.500
at a picture on Instagram but you look at
this picture and it has maybe two or three

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:18.470
likes and then your peer looks at the same
picture and now they see it and it has fifty

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:23.900
likes. How is this little bit of information,
how is this number going to change the way

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.590
that you respond to the picture. Are you more
likely to like it if it's popular and how

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.990
is it going to change the way that you take
in information, is your brain response to

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:37.570
that picture actually going to change? And
we zeroed in on this idea of the like because

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:40.290
it's something that really is brand new, right?

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:44.419
So for a very long time we've been telling
each other how we feel about information.

00:05:44.419 --> 00:05:49.139
If I appear in front of you and I'm telling
you a joke I get sort of an idea of your response

00:05:49.139 --> 00:05:52.970
based on you're smiling or your laughter.
But now we have this really interesting new

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:57.830
context where I'm getting a number that's
associated with something that I've created

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:01.840
or something I put out into the world. And
so what we found is that this little number,

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.900
the number of likes on a photo, affected how
likely teens were to like the pictures. So

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:12.169
they were significantly more likely to click
like, I use the word like a lot when I talk

00:06:12.169 --> 00:06:16.590
about this, they're significantly more likely
to click like on the picture if they believe

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:20.950
that many of their peers have also liked it.
So we get this peer influence is kind of happening

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:25.370
in a new way online. And then we also saw
that their brain response was changing that

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:30.139
when they looked at a picture that had a lot
of likes versus a few they showed more activation

00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:34.260
in a number of different parts of the brain
parts of the brain that are involved in social

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:38.639
cognition and thinking about your world parts
of the brain that are involved in visual perception,

00:06:38.639 --> 00:06:42.759
so even though they're seeing the same thing
that parts of the brain that process visual

00:06:42.759 --> 00:06:46.879
information are becoming more active which
suggests maybe they're scanning it a little

00:06:46.879 --> 00:06:50.430
more carefully, paying attention to it in
a different way. And then the really interesting

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:55.259
thing that we find is that parts of the brain
are involved in reward and reinforcement.

00:06:55.259 --> 00:06:58.659
So getting a reward of responding to that
reward being motivated to do something in

00:06:58.659 --> 00:07:02.360
the future. These parts of the brain were
becoming more active when they saw that a

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:06.460
picture had a lot of likes and especially
if they saw that their own picture had a lot

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:08.689
of likes as I'm sure you can imagine.

00:07:08.689 --> 00:07:15.009
So you're basically saying likes sort of like
chocolate or sex or crack will activate our

00:07:15.009 --> 00:07:21.099
dopamine reward system. And how is this new?
How is this different than what teenagers

00:07:21.099 --> 00:07:26.210
have been doing subtlety for thousands or
millennia?. So so what's particularly new

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:28.740
about digitality if that's a word?

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:32.940
Yeah, I mean that's kind of an open question
to some extent it is something that has already

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:37.310
existed. Right? So the idea that peers are
being influenced is absolutely not something

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:41.110
that is new. The idea that peers are very
motivated by what or that adolescents are

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:45.150
very motivated by what their peers think is
certainly something that already was happening

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:49.780
for a long time. And so some of the questions
that we're really interested in exploring

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:54.169
because again this is something we don't know
a lot about, is is this something that is

00:07:54.169 --> 00:07:58.259
really new? Or are we just seeing this kind
of recapitulation of what we already knew

00:07:58.259 --> 00:08:03.090
about adolescence. There are some things that
make social media very different. One is that

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:09.669
now there these numbers associated with information.
So the experience that you get of seeing that

00:08:09.669 --> 00:08:14.280
people are liking you is a little bit more
like a currency. Right now it's quantifiable.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:19.870
And so that's kind of the big question is
this in any way changing the experience of

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.270
getting information from your peers?

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:26.939
And what's great about your experiment was
that it was an experiment you actually manipulated

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:32.330
a number of likes that the photos got. So
you basically control the popularity.

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:33.370
Yes.

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:39.670
Currency there. But the cost of doing an experiment
is that you're seeing something in your fMRI

00:08:39.670 --> 00:08:45.310
or or any other measures in real time that
is perhaps a short lived effect.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:46.340
Yes

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:52.630
Richard, as a child analyst and psychiatrist
you're seeing sort of more globally the longer

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:58.320
term effect of the accumulation of these kind
of dopamine surges and disappointments and

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:03.520
so forth and multitasking in what it's doing
to our memory and all those kind of things.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:11.490
So are you seeing more ADHD, more social disorders,
more mutism. What are you seeing in your practice?

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:16.200
Well in fact I think what's really important
about these different forms of social of the

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:21.060
digital tools is that they are one of the
headlines in a way to be able to provide our

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:26.240
work is work oftentimes in terms of summarizing
what we have been finding out in the research.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:30.200
And I think it's really important for everybody
to think about them as tools that are for

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:38.320
communication, for entertainment and for education.
And that just like other tools they have advantages

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:42.060
and disadvantages. And I I think that some
of the debate that has been around about some

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:48.850
of the negative aspects of digital media and
digital tools is that there's like some global

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:53.780
effect that is all in one direction. So there's
the debate about the positives there's the

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:59.150
debate about the negatives and what's really
true is that people are pretty variable and

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:04.990
that there are positives and negatives and
it does impact people in different fashions.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:11.260
So that we do see that some persons,
in getting heavily involved with games do

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:16.940
end up having more difficulties with their
attention especially if they are engaged with

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:21.740
these activities at a young age. If they get
involved a lot of this activity is in fact

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:26.080
a dose response relationship, in some of the
studies there's an indication that if you're

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:31.140
involved with gaming for about an hour a day
it's going to enhance your attention. If it's

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:35.620
more than three hours a day then it starts
to deteriorate and it has an impact on lots

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:40.780
of other activities that require some attention.
The same thing can be true with social media.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:44.630
It depends on how you interpret the information.

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:49.500
Persons that get involved, there's a lot of
concern about Facebook and seeing how wonderful

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:55.440
everybody else is and like how diminished
your life is in comparison. If people approach

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.140
it with an envious way of dealing with things
it does result in more anxiety and depression

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:05.591
for adolescents. Than if people just say well
this is interesting. And is not going to be

00:11:05.591 --> 00:11:09.830
too concerned about where I where I stand.
So I think the headline I would like to present

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:15.450
is that we need to be careful we need gather
a lot more information to know about the positives,

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:19.310
negatives and also the impact it has on people
individually.

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:24.950
Just to push up on that one point you said.
So let's take the example of the kid who games

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:30.830
for more than three hours a day. How do we
know and let's say they exhibit signs of depression

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:37.020
or loneliness or inattention how do we know
that's not, the gaming is not the effect and

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:38.020
not the cause?

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:44.810
There's a there's a good number of longitudinal
studies. There clearly is an indication that

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:49.630
for example in clinical populations kids with
attention deficit hyperactivity disorder are

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:54.660
more prone to get involved with gaming. But
in terms of longitudinal studies looking at

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:58.950
kids at a one time point and a later time
point there is an indication that they get

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:05.240
more involved with problems with attention,
if you know after extensive amounts of use.

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:11.050
I'm also aware of another study maybe you
were involved in Florida where they they send

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:18.190
kids off to camp for like a little under a
week and one group they took away their media

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:22.570
and the other group they let them keep it
at the end, of course, the ones without the

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:27.960
media had a better time but they could also
actually perform better on measures of social

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:34.750
cognition, reading other people's emotions
and empathy et cetera. So you would that's

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:37.300
consistent with what you're seeing in the
clinic?

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:43.580
Well oftentimes yes. Now again there's also
benefits for some kids that are socially isolated.

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:48.030
For some kids that have a little bit of difficulty
with some of their social skills some aspects

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:51.840
of social media and some aspects of games
that involve some social interaction, especially

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:58.050
the multiplayer games, sometimes that's better.
That that sometimes results the kids sometimes

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:02.440
coming more out of their shell. And actually
establishing community across boundaries.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:06.750
Especially when they have very particular
interests.

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.180
Yeah.

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:12.870
You know Wikken golfers of the northeast Baltimore
are...OK.

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:18.000
I think Jan you have a more positive headline
for us or you were kind of mixed? So we're

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:21.060
going to end with the positive.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:27.680
As I was looking to everybody I tried to decide
if I should come down in a positive way.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:29.440
You're our only hope.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:34.700
Yes, Obi Wan. I'm going to, I'm going to start
with the positive side because we can always

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:36.750
go back to the negative side that's always
soeasy.

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:38.070
And I'm sure we will.

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:41.990
And I'm going to tell you why I want to start
with the positive side but let me start by

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:49.250
by saying my headline is. We live in an age
that is unprecedented as far as the educational

00:13:49.250 --> 00:13:54.190
opportunities are concerned that our children
have now. Not just in this country but all

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:59.361
over the world based on things that are designed
in this country and all over the world. And

00:13:59.361 --> 00:14:05.340
so I do not just mean that we have information
at our fingertips on the web. I also mean

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:09.780
that we have simulations in which you can
explore scenarios that you don't directly

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:15.550
have access to. We have games that can motivate
those who have decided to disengage from something

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:22.030
that they thought wasn't really of interest
to them. We have so many genres now of educational

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:27.190
environments available to us that teachers
nowadays are almost complaining about the

00:14:27.190 --> 00:14:30.960
wealth of different information and they don't
know how to choose from that. That did not

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:35.940
used to be the case when I was in school.
I very clearly remember not ever having any

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:43.860
of those tools and some of the adults in the
room probably that resonates with you.

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:48.420
So we have not only this unprecedented time
of wealth of information and high quality

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:53.480
information coming directly from the source,
from NASA from from all the from all different

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:58.530
kind of organizations that have done the research
and are presenting the results. But we also

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:05.300
are entering an age of well, shall I say accountability.
But what I mean is that is an age where there's

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:11.680
a quantification in a positive sense of what
how we're actually doing. And so what we're

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:15.600
seeing is, there was first the quantified
self movement in terms of how you know how

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:21.230
many steps you took today which is exciting.
Or I know how many miles I ran or bike this

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:26.560
week, which is also exciting but we're starting
to see systems, adaptive learning engines

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:30.650
that come out that tell you what your memory
strength is about what you've learned. What

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:35.580
you're good at what you are not good at. And
so we we're entering this age where we get

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:41.640
feedback about our learning personalized,
not as a group which is easy right, but personalized

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:48.690
that open a number of opportunities that we're
only barely starting to understand.

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:53.410
And so I said I would give you an example.
I'm going to keep it short. We just finished

00:15:53.410 --> 00:16:01.191
a study with Syrian refugees in Turkey. As
you know there's a huge crisis in Syria. Lots

00:16:01.191 --> 00:16:05.200
of families millions had to leave their homes.
Turkey is one of the countries that took in

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:11.140
the most of of those refugees and they're
living in camps. So the opportunities to learn

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:15.560
would have been very very limited had it not
been for digital media. So we were asked to

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:20.010
help out and we designed a four week curriculum
where they studied for two hours a day five

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:27.100
days a week for four weeks. We taught them
how to code. We taught them how to learn Turkish,

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:32.340
because you cannot get into school if you
don't know the language. We added cognitive

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:38.850
skills training especially executive functions
training. And because there's a lot of hopelessness

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:42.760
and lack of positive outlook we added a game
that actually gives you full control over

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:48.110
your own environment. Some of you guys might
be playing it, Minecraft. And so. So we had

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:53.940
this curriculum that is largely based on entirely
based on digital media and largely on games.

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:58.450
And we saw after four weeks so many positive
indications of having a positive impact on

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:03.280
that that we're now seeing to to scale this
up. And so if one wants to look on the positive

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:07.000
side those are some of the examples that I
would want to give.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:12.589
I mean I volunteer for a online only tuition
free university called University of the People

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:18.510
that's trying to reach the same underserved
populations. But you're also teaching at NYU

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:25.329
and you're teaching both digitally only and
in the classroom in the brick and mortar sense.

00:17:25.329 --> 00:17:31.929
What are the take home headline lessons, if
someone was going to try to develop pedagogical

00:17:31.929 --> 00:17:34.370
games online?

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:42.429
Yeah that's a that's a really loaded question.
Let me start by saying that I do not believe

00:17:42.429 --> 00:17:46.910
that entire curriculum should be game based.
Recently we've used games for brain training.

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:53.169
Kind of a topic that is a little bit controversial
ever since the Luminosity got fined $52 million

00:17:53.169 --> 00:17:58.820
for making false claims about how effective
they are. So we decided well let's look at

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:03.129
that as researchers right. We were interested
in that, executive functions as underlying

00:18:03.129 --> 00:18:08.289
almost everything we do. Right now you're
using all of your executive functions. You're

00:18:08.289 --> 00:18:13.110
inhibiting the desire to do something else.
Perhaps you're using your working memory.

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:17.250
Your the shifting function is when different
people talk and have different ideas then

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:21.309
you shift and that's cognitive flexibility
involved. So it's something that we do for

00:18:21.309 --> 00:18:25.809
almost everything we do of thought and action
and emotion and it can be trained. That's

00:18:25.809 --> 00:18:26.809
the good news.

00:18:26.809 --> 00:18:32.780
It's also something that as students, children
who don't have parents who can spend a lot

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:36.860
of time with them because they're need to
work two or three jobs. They don't get the

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:42.049
opportunities to train the executive functions,
which is one reason why we see an achievement

00:18:42.049 --> 00:18:46.529
gap in schools. So what if you had a game
that could train your executive functions

00:18:46.529 --> 00:18:51.030
and was actually a fun game to play. And so
we built some of those. We're testing them.

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:54.809
What we're doing is design research so we
compare two different versions we say here.

00:18:54.809 --> 00:19:01.230
You know we actually worked with 11 to or
12 to 15-16 year olds a lot. We give them

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:05.909
the games and they play two different versions.
One might be adaptive. One might not be. One

00:19:05.909 --> 00:19:09.679
has different game characters than the other
etc.. And then we look at which one works

00:19:09.679 --> 00:19:14.570
better. And in the end we see what the overall
outcome is on academic achievement. Does it

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:20.399
actually enhance your performance in school
etc. and if that's all the case then they're

00:19:20.399 --> 00:19:24.179
all available for free on our website. You
can play them at your heart's desire and see

00:19:24.179 --> 00:19:26.419
if that works for you.

00:19:26.419 --> 00:19:31.299
That's I think it was too much positive. Now
we tried.

00:19:31.299 --> 00:19:37.820
A few years ago there was a study at UCLA
that divided people into learning something

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:43.750
while they were multitasking. And the other
group of course, were directed to have their

00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:51.309
full attention on on not on the task of learning,
of memorizing something. And not only did

00:19:51.309 --> 00:19:56.200
the ones that gave their full attention of
course do better on recall and in learning

00:19:56.200 --> 00:20:03.870
a task they actually encoded in their brain
differently. So they, the multi-taskers they

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:10.000
found that there's striatum was more activated
and it was encoded as procedural memory which

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:15.690
is like driving a car where it's a habit,
but you're not as conscious of it. And the

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:20.830
people have focused store the new knowledge
and the new skill as declarative memory and

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:26.070
the hippocampus and the benefit of declarative
memory is that it allows you to abstract from

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:29.809
the particular to the general. So if you want
to drive a car maybe you don't want to drive

00:20:29.809 --> 00:20:36.830
a motorcycle or a plane but if you know how
to solve a proof of you know partial differential

00:20:36.830 --> 00:20:42.279
equation you might be able to transfer that
to something else that has the same basic

00:20:42.279 --> 00:20:45.419
logical framework.

00:20:45.419 --> 00:20:51.100
So are we all, Lauren, I’m looking at you
you but anyone can jump in. I mean one of

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:55.780
the consequences of the enormous number of
hours that we have our devices with us and

00:20:55.780 --> 00:21:01.320
are on them and particularly young people
are we increasingly becoming kind of rote

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:07.009
machines where we learn how to do things through
procedural memory and that doesn't sound really

00:21:07.009 --> 00:21:12.460
great for the age of the coming bots where
