00:28:32.029 --> 00:28:35.999
attention problems and trying to see what
happens if they listened to music as they're

00:28:35.999 --> 00:28:41.919
doing their work. If they select the music,
they actually improve their productivity.

00:28:41.919 --> 00:28:45.970
It seems that kind of the brain is multitasking.
There’s one part that's kind of attending

00:28:45.970 --> 00:28:49.590
to the music sort of like activating the brain
and making it like .....

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:51.539
Keeping it occupied? that part occupied?

00:28:51.539 --> 00:28:54.739
Kind of keeping it occupied, more like an
idling in the car. The car is ready to move

00:28:54.739 --> 00:28:59.149
up and to advance when they present to a problem.
And the same thing has been fun with adults

00:28:59.149 --> 00:29:01.650
when they're involved with doodling and listening
to lectures

00:29:01.650 --> 00:29:03.279
That's the fidget spinner right?

00:29:03.279 --> 00:29:04.279
Well it is.

00:29:04.279 --> 00:29:09.220
The idea is like you're doing something you're
taking up part of that energy.

00:29:09.220 --> 00:29:14.869
Yeah, but like doodling does results in people
paying better attention to lectures that are

00:29:14.869 --> 00:29:20.860
being presented. So I think that to be really
careful of these definitions because we can't

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:23.470
get overly broad in that regard and get confused.

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:30.220
So you're in the business of actually giving
best practices to to families the parents

00:29:30.220 --> 00:29:36.989
of how to manage this brave new world that's
exploding upon us. So what are some of the

00:29:36.989 --> 00:29:41.279
points to what are some of the basic things
that given all this kind of context specificity

00:29:41.279 --> 00:29:46.169
on the one hand, on the other hand what do
you tell us to do with our kids?

00:29:46.169 --> 00:29:51.200
Well I think one thing that's really important
is to be a bit more very conscious of what

00:29:51.200 --> 00:29:57.159
we are talking about to be really tuned into
it and not. I think one thing that has happened

00:29:57.159 --> 00:30:02.489
is that we think that this technology is not
understandable. And that it's like so brand

00:30:02.489 --> 00:30:07.340
new that it can't be put into a context and
perhaps I'm being too simplistic and maybe

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:12.350
that's the way my mind works. But I do think
that it's really helpful for people to say,

00:30:12.350 --> 00:30:17.129
well many of these instruments are a means
of communication. You know so what do you

00:30:17.129 --> 00:30:21.200
think about the role of communication in your
children's lives and what kind of communication

00:30:21.200 --> 00:30:26.071
do you want them to have? There's they're
tools for education. What kind of education

00:30:26.071 --> 00:30:31.309
what kind of content do you want to have your
kids learn about and what what methods, what

00:30:31.309 --> 00:30:35.641
things you want to be involved with in terms
of leisure? And I like to be able to suggest

00:30:35.641 --> 00:30:40.309
to parents that they think about you know,
for example, with education you can use digital

00:30:40.309 --> 00:30:44.940
resources to be able to learn how to be you
know some really problematic issues. You can

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:48.881
you can learn to be a really good anorexic.
You can learn to be a good bomb maker. You

00:30:48.881 --> 00:30:53.230
can also do all the different kinds of fascinating
things have been presented here already.

00:30:53.230 --> 00:30:56.970
With communication you can get really good
at being a bully. You can also get really

00:30:56.970 --> 00:31:01.830
good at being able to communicate to people
in Alaska and you know in Africa and many

00:31:01.830 --> 00:31:06.549
other communities that you can really make
a good set of connections. And with leisure

00:31:06.549 --> 00:31:10.059
time you can spend you know most of your time
on junk and things that are not going to be

00:31:10.059 --> 00:31:14.970
very, very useful or you could do it on some
things that are fulfilling and a little bit

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:18.919
of junk. And so I think when parents do that
and think about these things as similar to

00:31:18.919 --> 00:31:24.999
what they were presented as kids. And I think
that helps kind of like put a context into

00:31:24.999 --> 00:31:26.559
that's really very useful.

00:31:26.559 --> 00:31:33.019
How how is a parent to control all of this?
I mean, Dana Boyd at Microsoft Research argues

00:31:33.019 --> 00:31:40.859
that actually all of the stuff like the specific
teen talk that parents can't decode on social

00:31:40.859 --> 00:31:46.179
media sites and so forth, is all response
to the fact that we over program and over

00:31:46.179 --> 00:31:52.169
control our kids today. Not an effect but
actually, sorry not actually cause but the

00:31:52.169 --> 00:31:58.840
effect of what we're doing already in trying
to kind of control their worlds. I mean I'm

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:07.399
I'm I'm not only de-friended from my son's
Facebook I'm blocked. So what am I to do it?

00:32:07.399 --> 00:32:15.350
I think the thing is, you know kids and adolescents
have always found some way to keep things

00:32:15.350 --> 00:32:21.899
secret. The question is like how secret and
how problematic. You know I work in a clinical

00:32:21.899 --> 00:32:28.009
setting. You don't want to be the parent of,
and this is a number of years ago when things

00:32:28.009 --> 00:32:33.850
were just starting, to find out that your
twelve year old daughter has gotten really

00:32:33.850 --> 00:32:38.039
obsessed with something and really concerned
showing a lot of signs of anxiety because

00:32:38.039 --> 00:32:44.129
what has happened is she met somebody at camp.
The boy's been sexting with her and that she's

00:32:44.129 --> 00:32:48.010
like now preoccupied and she doesn't know
how to stop it. And you don't want to be the

00:32:48.010 --> 00:32:52.299
parent that finds out that when you go to
Verizon and find out, OK here's the series

00:32:52.299 --> 00:32:56.240
of texts that has been going on for months.
You don't want to be the parent that finds

00:32:56.240 --> 00:33:01.799
out that your teenage son, and these these
are cases, has been involved with staying

00:33:01.799 --> 00:33:08.590
up and chatting and texting into, you know,
five in the morning with a girl who's suicidal.

00:33:08.590 --> 00:33:11.480
You want to be able to have some sense of
what is going on. You don't even know each

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:13.809
and every detail you get to know the trends.

00:33:13.809 --> 00:33:16.639
That sounds good but how does one effect that?

00:33:16.639 --> 00:33:20.450
Well I think what you do is you do kind of
like recognize that these again are tools

00:33:20.450 --> 00:33:25.460
you know like the example I gave in my talks
is that you know we have chainsaws. Chainsaws

00:33:25.460 --> 00:33:28.749
are very useful. They're very helpful. They
help us do a lot of different things. But

00:33:28.749 --> 00:33:34.210
then you have the Texas Chainsaw Massacre,
can be used in a different way. Not to just

00:33:34.210 --> 00:33:38.509
think about it as being something that you
can't step into. Not to think that like OK

00:33:38.509 --> 00:33:43.389
every kid, and I've had some situations where
people were saying eight year olds having

00:33:43.389 --> 00:33:48.419
an inalienable right to privacy. Well really?
Did you grow up that way? Did your parents

00:33:48.419 --> 00:33:52.289
raise you that way? I mean we've been parenting
for a long time. And I think we have to try

00:33:52.289 --> 00:33:57.159
to be logical and reasonable to say, well
I do need to know a little bit of the trend.

00:33:57.159 --> 00:34:02.080
I do need to know what's going on. I do need
to be tuned in enough to be aware of what

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:03.080
you're doing.

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:07.450
And the technology tools that we sometimes
feel are so overwhelming that we don't even

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:12.090
want to start to understand what our children
are doing can also be used to help with that.

00:34:12.090 --> 00:34:16.600
Right? So I have my favorite app on my phone
is the one that lets me turn off the Wi-Fi

