00:52:02.110 --> 00:52:07.770
say there are things that intrude in what
used to be family life or life in general

00:52:07.770 --> 00:52:12.690
that we really have to ask ourselves is that
worth giving up? Or. Or is that really something

00:52:12.690 --> 00:52:18.580
that we want to retain? And obviously opinions
will vary on what we can and shouldn't give

00:52:18.580 --> 00:52:25.230
up. But people walk into traffic to pedestrian
traffic deaths up by 10 percent or so on.

00:52:25.230 --> 00:52:29.590
And people attribute that to people just staring
at their phones, And if just walk around and

00:52:29.590 --> 00:52:35.970
you'll see a lot of examples of that people
will sit in restaurants across from each other

00:52:35.970 --> 00:52:40.320
both on their phones and deeply engaged in
whatever they're doing just not with each

00:52:40.320 --> 00:52:46.420
other. And so where do we want to push back
as a culture as a society as parents and say

00:52:46.420 --> 00:52:52.870
when, we still can, and say look this is this
is something that we don't want to have. Right?

00:52:52.870 --> 00:52:57.840
And it's very very hard but we can't give
up. We have to we have to keep fighting the

00:52:57.840 --> 00:52:58.840
good fight.

00:52:58.840 --> 00:53:03.130
I think that debates and then these discussions
I think are we we're at a phase where we are

00:53:03.130 --> 00:53:09.630
recognizing that these are important and powerful
tools but I also think that those debates

00:53:09.630 --> 00:53:14.830
and these concerns are letting us become sort
of like catching up with technology. And I

00:53:14.830 --> 00:53:18.950
think if we look at history that is the way
that it goes. And then when something new

00:53:18.950 --> 00:53:23.730
is brought out there is a little bit of time
and there is a gap between like really learning

00:53:23.730 --> 00:53:29.130
how to use it well and effectively. And one
part I'd like to also bring out is not only

00:53:29.130 --> 00:53:34.210
that working in teaching kids about using
things effectively well and having them be

00:53:34.210 --> 00:53:39.930
cautious. I do think, from the perspective
of parents and adults we have to do the same.

00:53:39.930 --> 00:53:46.050
Because there is a good amount of indication
that when adults are not using these materials

00:53:46.050 --> 00:53:51.220
appropriately, indirectly they're also harming
their kids. There's a there's a fair amount

00:53:51.220 --> 00:53:55.420
of studies that are being looked at right
now with regard to parents use of phones and

00:53:55.420 --> 00:54:02.030
in connection with connecting with their infant
kids and kind of like giving their kids these

00:54:02.030 --> 00:54:06.480
these tools too early so they don't get advantages.

00:54:06.480 --> 00:54:10.071
On that note I want to ask one last question
Richard, before we go to the audience, which

00:54:10.071 --> 00:54:16.920
is that the American Society of pediatricians
recently revised their regulations used to

00:54:16.920 --> 00:54:24.230
be zero digital media for those under two
years of age and now they've relaxed that,

00:54:24.230 --> 00:54:25.880
is that the right policy?

00:54:25.880 --> 00:54:31.580
I think so I think it's well founded in reason.
All the concern had been based upon work that

00:54:31.580 --> 00:54:37.310
had been done that found that kids at this
early age would inhibit their attention and

00:54:37.310 --> 00:54:42.450
their capacity to be able to pay attention.
But more subsequent studies indicated that,

00:54:42.450 --> 00:54:47.750
well it's so it's within reason it's fine.
I think that is what is happening. We're coming

00:54:47.750 --> 00:54:51.780
to reasonable understanding how to use things
and I think that that's what happened with

00:54:51.780 --> 00:54:56.370
the Academy of Pediatrics. And I also suspect
that part of what happened because many of

00:54:56.370 --> 00:55:02.550
the people involved as they got younger cohort
started to say well this really doesn't seem

00:55:02.550 --> 00:55:07.070
like it could actually happen. So what what
what what can really be reasonable ways?

00:55:07.070 --> 00:55:11.520
And they're concerned that kids who don't
learn these technologies at that age are really

00:55:11.520 --> 00:55:12.520
behind their peers?

00:55:12.520 --> 00:55:17.770
Exactly. Exactly right. There'll be a digital
divide which would be inappropriate as well.

00:55:17.770 --> 00:55:31.210
So we have 10 minutes for questions from the
audience. Also in the back there.

00:55:31.210 --> 00:55:39.200
This is more about adults and social media
I suppose. But, I asked my dad thus a long

00:55:39.200 --> 00:55:44.120
time ago, whether, because he had just gotten
on to Facebook and was talking to people he

00:55:44.120 --> 00:55:50.790
hadn’t talked to in twenty years, they both
immigrated ages ago. And I asked whether it

00:55:50.790 --> 00:55:56.140
was better to forget about all those friends,
you had met twenty years ago and let them

00:55:56.140 --> 00:56:01.460
fade out of your memory like you would before
social media. Or is better to check in, you

00:56:01.460 --> 00:56:08.660
know this shallow bits of lives without being
involved with them. Because ultimately which

00:56:08.660 --> 00:56:14.840
is more painful? Which is sadder and he never
really responded an answer to that. So I always

00:56:14.840 --> 00:56:22.980
wondered from Neuroscience perspective what
works? Is it better to a social, shallow understanding

00:56:22.980 --> 00:56:28.400
of other people's lives or just let them fade
away?

00:56:28.400 --> 00:56:33.880
So I think the question you are asking. You're
talking about a value judgment right? Is it

00:56:33.880 --> 00:56:38.480
better or is it worse. And this comes up again
and again is there is never going to be this

00:56:38.480 --> 00:56:44.380
universal idea of what is better or worse
if you're talking about emotions or your particular

00:56:44.380 --> 00:56:48.920
experience that you have there maybe people
for which having those connections long term

00:56:48.920 --> 00:56:53.920
being able to maintain them having, though
it may be shallow continue in connection with

00:56:53.920 --> 00:56:58.260
someone is better or worse. I think there's
this hope that we have that we can look in

00:56:58.260 --> 00:57:04.510
the brain and suddenly become objective. Right?
Is that because it's science because it's

00:57:04.510 --> 00:57:08.010
something that's physical or biological we
can use all these words and say all right

00:57:08.010 --> 00:57:12.290
now we're getting from this kind of touchy
feely psychology stuff where it's just whatever

00:57:12.290 --> 00:57:16.550
you think you are. Now we're going to look
in the brain and unfortunately that or maybe

00:57:16.550 --> 00:57:20.020
fortunately, I don't know if it's unfortunate
but it's just not how the brain works I mean

00:57:20.020 --> 00:57:25.450
the one thing I will say that's really incredible
about our brains is that they're very good

00:57:25.450 --> 00:57:29.950
at adapting to all sorts of different situations
and, in fact, there are a lot of developmental

00:57:29.950 --> 00:57:34.570
cognitive scientists and I'm one of them who
think that there's something really special

00:57:34.570 --> 00:57:40.440
about the adolescent period that our brain
is very sensitive to our social sphere because

00:57:40.440 --> 00:57:44.780
we're learning about our particular culture.
And so something that's going to be valued

00:57:44.780 --> 00:57:49.540
very highly in my culture the number of friends
that I have. The way that I interact with

00:57:49.540 --> 00:57:53.670
people may be very different from what is
valued in another culture. And it's this wonderful

00:57:53.670 --> 00:57:57.960
thing that our brains allow us to figure that
out. It can be very tough when as you age

00:57:57.960 --> 00:58:01.571
your culture starts to change and the way
that you grew up is now very different. But

00:58:01.571 --> 00:58:06.870
all of that is to say that the brain is never
going to tell you whether it's better or worse.

00:58:06.870 --> 00:58:11.490
And in fact for these questions sometimes
it's better to ask yourself or to ask somebody

00:58:11.490 --> 00:58:16.000
else sometimes. What we think of as being
more touchy feely is actually going to have

00:58:16.000 --> 00:58:22.660
more of a direct effect on our outcomes and
how to guide our behavior a little better.

00:58:22.660 --> 00:58:26.760
Time for one last question.

00:58:26.760 --> 00:58:30.380
My question is you talk about with a lot,
a of conversations has been rear view kind

00:58:30.380 --> 00:58:34.780
of taking this to this point but if you just
roll over what would be the headline ten years

00:58:34.780 --> 00:58:36.231
from now? What’s the impact on learning
and how we behave? One last point, they are

00:58:36.231 --> 00:58:37.231
showing or some study is showing that the
psychological profile of 16 year old the 25

00:58:37.231 --> 00:58:38.231
year old is almost actually the same. That's
one of the changes, right. So going forward

00:58:38.231 --> 00:58:39.231
what was the headline? If you take your conversation
for roll it forward a for a few years. What's

00:58:39.231 --> 00:58:40.231
the impact on us and how things can change.
From a learning perspective?

00:58:40.231 --> 00:58:43.670
I’ll take a first shot, I'd rather not go
ten years because I don't know what I could

00:58:43.670 --> 00:58:50.510
see but five years perhaps and I'll start
with the example of digital versus print.

00:58:50.510 --> 00:58:58.560
It was widely predicted around 2011, 2012
that print really was going to fade. And what

00:58:58.560 --> 00:59:03.810
has happened instead is we've really reached
what's known as a both-and world. There are

00:59:03.810 --> 00:59:08.270
certain things that we do digitally all the
time the certain things that we are doing

00:59:08.270 --> 00:59:13.290
a lot in print and that's not just one person
or another person it's independent bookstores

00:59:13.290 --> 00:59:18.220
are coming back and if you look at sales figures
and growth levels for digital books versus

00:59:18.220 --> 00:59:24.260
print books, print books has a higher growth
rate than digital does. And audio is way higher

00:59:24.260 --> 00:59:29.600
than both of them. I think the same thing
is going to happen and maybe it's because

00:59:29.600 --> 00:59:36.050
of the settling in period for are coming to
grips with, we get to control what we do with

00:59:36.050 --> 00:59:43.810
the devices. We get to control what the devices
are. We get to make our own decisions. I think

00:59:43.810 --> 00:59:49.000
we're going to get tired of being shallow.
We know that people who spend a lot of time

00:59:49.000 --> 00:59:53.220
on Facebook often either feel depressed because
everybody looked better than they did or so

00:59:53.220 --> 01:00:00.410
they think or what came from those three hours?
Zilch. And at some point you know our education

01:00:00.410 --> 01:00:05.840
patterns change or social patterns change.
And I think at least within five years we're

01:00:05.840 --> 01:00:10.280
going to see more of a balance and more a
feeling that we're in control of what we're

01:00:10.280 --> 01:00:14.160
doing and we'll have a range of choices and
feel that we have the right to make those

01:00:14.160 --> 01:00:15.160
choices.

01:00:15.160 --> 01:00:20.360
I love that that as an answer. But I think
in five years we will have had two more waves

01:00:20.360 --> 01:00:26.980
of new technologies that all promise to solve
all of our right. And that then only ended

01:00:26.980 --> 01:00:31.960
up adding to what we have available at our
fingertips that in our arsenal is just if

01:00:31.960 --> 01:00:38.760
you want. now have to choose which way of
teaching and learning is the best way for

01:00:38.760 --> 01:00:45.560
me for particular subject for particular context
etc. so I am unfortunate as much love your

01:00:45.560 --> 01:00:50.390
vision more than mine I think we are going
to be faced with this ever changing technological

01:00:50.390 --> 01:00:57.410
world we live in. Where where new interventions
and inventions will come out that will challenge

01:00:57.410 --> 01:01:03.200
us. And so we need to be up for the tasks
we need to to be ready for that and how to

01:01:03.200 --> 01:01:05.890
do that would be probably a topic for another
panel.

01:01:05.890 --> 01:01:12.520
I think it's important also to say that technology
is not its own realm sui generis or isolated

01:01:12.520 --> 01:01:19.090
from other changes going on in society whether
that's changing age structures and in society

01:01:19.090 --> 01:01:25.670
as we age because of low fertility rate or
rising economic inequality the changing nature

01:01:25.670 --> 01:01:31.370
of work and prolonged adolescence which is
symbolized by Obamacare where kids can stay

01:01:31.370 --> 01:01:38.550
on their their parents health insurance till
25 or 26. So we can't talk about the future.

01:01:38.550 --> 01:01:44.050
It's hard enough to read and present. It's
almost impossible to talk about the future

01:01:44.050 --> 01:01:51.370
at all. Certainly in isolation from from other
forces that are at work and we can't predict.

01:01:51.370 --> 01:01:52.810
So thank you to everyone for attending this
evening. Thank you again everybody.

